<http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/QuantityKindDimensionVector> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "<p class=\"lm-para\">A  <em>Quantity Kind Dimension Vector</em> describes the dimensionality of a quantity kind in the context of a system of units. In the SI system of units, the dimensions of a quantity kind are expressed as a product of the basic physical dimensions mass (\\\\(M\\\\)), length (\\\\(L\\\\)), time (\\\\(T\\\\)) current (\\\\(I\\\\)), amount of substance (\\\\(N\\\\)), luminous intensity (\\\\(J\\\\)) and absolute temperature (\\\\(\\\theta\\\\)) as \\\\(dim \\\\, Q = L^{\\\\alpha} \\\\, M^{\\eta} \\\\, T^{\\\\gamma} \\\\, I ^{\\\\delta} \\\\, \\\theta ^{\\\\epsilon} \\\\, N^{\\\\eta} \\\\, J ^{\\\nu}\\\\).</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"lm-para\">The rational powers of the dimensional exponents, \\\\(\\\\alpha, \\\\, \\eta, \\\\, \\\\gamma, \\\\, \\\\delta, \\\\, \\\\epsilon, \\\\, \\\\eta, \\\\, \\\nu\\\\), are positive, negative, or zero.</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"lm-para\">For example, the dimension of the physical quantity kind \\\\(\\\\it{speed}\\\\) is \\\\(\\oxed{length/time}\\\\), \\\\(L/T\\\\) or \\\\(LT^{-1}\\\\), and the dimension of the physical quantity kind force is \\\\(\\oxed{mass \\\times acceleration}\\\\) or \\\\(\\oxed{mass \\\times (length/time)/time}\\\\), \\\\(ML/T^2\\\\) or \\\\(MLT^{-2}\\\\) respectively.</p>"^^<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML> .
<http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/QuantityKindDimensionVector> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:B2b237b704e6dd33b75cc0a1322c9a2f6 .
<http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/QuantityKindDimensionVector> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> _:B8ebb600de54ed8c23acc12659db7bcf9 .
<http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/QuantityKindDimensionVector> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt> .
